\section{Introduction}\label{sec:ct-intro}

Compilers are among the most complex software systems ever built.  A modern
optimizing compiler such as GCC or LLVM contains millions of lines of code,
implements hundreds of optimization passes, and must handle the full complexity
of languages like C, C++, and Rust.  Given this complexity, compiler bugs are
inevitable---and their consequences can be severe.  A miscompilation can
introduce silent data corruption, security vulnerabilities, or incorrect
behavior in any program compiled by the buggy compiler.

Unlike application software, where bugs affect individual users, a compiler bug
affects \emph{every program} compiled by the buggy version.  The infamous
``GoingSpace'' bug in GCC~\cite{regehr2014compiler} silently generated incorrect
code for certain loop patterns, potentially affecting thousands of applications.

Testing compilers presents unique challenges:
\begin{itemize}
\item The input space (all valid programs) is infinite.
\item The expected output (correct compiled code) is difficult to determine
      automatically.
\item Compiler bugs are often triggered by unusual combinations of language
      features, making them hard to find with hand-written tests.
\end{itemize}

This chapter surveys the state of the art in compiler testing, from differential
testing (\cref{sec:ct-differential}) and metamorphic testing
(\cref{sec:ct-metamorphic}) to random program generation
(\cref{sec:ct-random}) and fuzzing (\cref{sec:ct-fuzzing}).  We discuss test
case reduction (\cref{sec:ct-reduction}) and survey the major tools
(\cref{sec:ct-tools}).

% ============================================================
% Compiler Correctness and Bugs
% ============================================================
\section{Compiler Correctness and Bugs}\label{sec:ct-correctness}

\subsection{Types of Compiler Bugs}\label{sec:ct-bug-types}

Compiler bugs can be classified into several categories:

\begin{definition}[Compiler Bug Classification]\label{def:bug-class}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Crash bugs (ICE):} The compiler crashes (internal compiler error)
      on a valid input.  These are the easiest to detect and the least harmful.
\item \textbf{Miscompilation bugs:} The compiler produces incorrect code for a
      valid input.  These are the most dangerous, as they can introduce silent
      errors in compiled programs.
\item \textbf{Diagnostic bugs:} The compiler produces incorrect error messages
      or fails to report errors for invalid programs.
\item \textbf{Performance bugs:} The compiler produces correct but suboptimal
      code, missing optimization opportunities.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Why Compilers Are Hard to Test}\label{sec:ct-hard}

Testing compilers is fundamentally challenging for several reasons:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Infinite input space:} The set of valid programs is infinite, so
      exhaustive testing is impossible.
\item \textbf{The oracle problem:} Given a program, it is undecidable in general
      whether the compiled code is correct.  This is the classic
      \emph{test oracle problem}~\cite{orso2004oracle}.
\item \textbf{Complex interactions:} Bugs often arise from interactions between
      optimization passes, language features, and target architectures.
\item \textbf{Undefined behavior:} In languages like C and C++, programs with
      undefined behavior can be compiled to any code; distinguishing a
      miscompilation from a program with UB requires careful analysis.
\end{itemize}

