\section{Differential Testing}\label{sec:ct-differential}

\subsection{The Differential Testing Approach}\label{sec:ct-diff-approach}

\emph{Differential testing}~\cite{mckeeman1998differential} compares the
behavior of multiple implementations of the same specification.  If two
compilers produce different outputs for the same input, at least one of them
has a bug.

\begin{definition}[Differential Testing]\label{def:diff-test}
Given $n$ implementations $I_1, \ldots, I_n$ of a language specification and a
test input $t$, differential testing checks whether:
\[
I_1(t) = I_2(t) = \cdots = I_n(t)
\]
If the outputs differ, a \emph{discrepancy} is reported, indicating a potential
bug in at least one implementation.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Applying Differential Testing to Compilers}\label{sec:ct-diff-compilers}

For compilers, differential testing can be applied at several levels:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Cross-compiler:} Compile the same program with different compilers
      (e.g., GCC and Clang) and compare the outputs.
\item \textbf{Cross-optimization:} Compile the same program with different
      optimization levels (e.g., \texttt{-O0} and \texttt{-O3}) and compare the
      outputs.
\item \textbf{Cross-version:} Compile the same program with different versions
      of the same compiler and compare the outputs.
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Differential Testing of Compilers}\label{alg:diff-test}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program $P$, compilers $C_1, C_2$
\Ensure Discrepancy report or ``consistent''
\State $O_1 \gets C_1(P)$ \Comment{Compile with $C_1$}
\State $O_2 \gets C_2(P)$ \Comment{Compile with $C_2$}
\State $R_1 \gets \mathit{run}(O_1)$ \Comment{Execute output of $C_1$}
\State $R_2 \gets \mathit{run}(O_2)$ \Comment{Execute output of $C_2$}
\If{$R_1 \neq R_2$}
  \State Report discrepancy: $R_1$ vs.\ $R_2$
  \State \Comment{Determine which compiler is buggy (requires oracle)}
\Else
  \State \Return ``consistent''
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Challenges of Differential Testing}\label{sec:ct-diff-challenges}

Differential testing faces several challenges:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Undefined behavior:} If the program has undefined behavior,
      different compilers are free to produce different results.  The discrepancy
      is not a bug.
\item \textbf{Unspecified behavior:} Some language features have unspecified
      (but defined) behavior, where the implementation may choose among several
      options.
\item \textbf{Non-determinism:} Some programs have non-deterministic behavior
      (e.g., reading uninitialized memory, concurrent programs without
      synchronization).
\item \textbf{Oracle problem:} When a discrepancy is found, determining which
      compiler is wrong requires additional analysis.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{YarpGen and CSmith}\label{sec:ct-yarpgen-csmith}

\textsc{CSmith}~\cite{yang2011finding} is a random C program generator designed
specifically for differential testing of compilers.  It generates programs that
avoid undefined behavior (by construction) and have well-defined outputs that
can be compared across compilers.  CSmith has found hundreds of bugs in GCC,
Clang, and other compilers.

\textsc{YarpGen}~\cite{yarpgen2020} is a similar tool that generates programs
for testing C++ compilers, with a focus on triggering optimization bugs.

