\section{Metamorphic Testing}\label{sec:ct-metamorphic}

\subsection{The Metamorphic Approach}\label{sec:ct-meta-approach}

\emph{Metamorphic testing}~\cite{chen2020metamorphic,chen2018metamorphic} is a
property-based testing technique that checks whether certain
\emph{metamorphic relations} hold between the outputs of related inputs.

\begin{definition}[Metamorphic Relation]\label{def:metamorphic-relation}
A \emph{metamorphic relation} (MR) for a program $P$ is a relation $R$ between
inputs and outputs such that for all inputs $x_1, x_2$:
\[
(x_1, x_2) \in R_{\mathit{input}} \implies (P(x_1), P(x_2)) \in R_{\mathit{output}}
\]
where $R_{\mathit{input}}$ is a relation on inputs and $R_{\mathit{output}}$ is
a relation on outputs.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Metamorphic Relations for Compilers}\label{sec:ct-meta-compilers}

For compilers, metamorphic relations express properties that should hold across
different compilations of related programs:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Equivalence MR:} If $P_1 \equiv P_2$ (semantically equivalent),
      then $\mathit{compile}(P_1) \equiv \mathit{compile}(P_2)$ (produce
      equivalent code).
\item \textbf{Optimization MR:} If $P_2$ is obtained from $P_1$ by a semantics-
      preserving transformation, then $\mathit{compile}(P_1, \mathit{-O3})$ and
      $\mathit{compile}(P_2, \mathit{-O3})$ should produce the same output.
\item \textbf{Inlining MR:} Inlining a function call should not change the
      program's behavior.
\item \textbf{Dead code MR:} Adding unreachable code should not change the
      program's behavior.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Metamorphic Testing of a Compiler]\label{ex:meta-compiler}
Consider the metamorphic relation: ``Adding a dead store to a local variable
should not change the program's output.''  We generate a program $P$, compute
its output $O_1$, then create $P'$ by adding a dead store, and compute its
output $O_2$.  If $O_1 \neq O_2$, the compiler has a bug.
\end{example}

\subsection{Fuzzing with Metamorphic Relations}\label{sec:ct-meta-fuzzing}

Metamorphic testing can be combined with fuzzing to automatically generate test
cases and check metamorphic relations:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Metamorphic Fuzzing of Compilers}\label{alg:meta-fuzz}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program generator $G$, metamorphic relation $R$, compiler $C$
\Ensure Bug reports
\Loop
  \State $P \gets G()$ \Comment{Generate random program}
  \State $P' \gets R_{\mathit{transform}}(P)$ \Comment{Apply metamorphic transformation}
  \State $O_1 \gets \mathit{run}(C(P))$
  \State $O_2 \gets \mathit{run}(C(P'))$
  \If{$\neg R_{\mathit{output}}(O_1, O_2)$}
    \State Report bug: MR violation
  \EndIf
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{EMI Testing}\label{sec:ct-emi}

\emph{EMI} (Error-Masking Injection) testing~\cite{chen2018metamorphic} is a
specific form of metamorphic testing for compilers.  The key idea is to
transform a program by injecting code that should have no effect (e.g., dead
code, redundant computations) and check that the output is unchanged.

The \textsc{CSmith-EMI} and \textsc{YarpGen-EMI} tools implement EMI testing
for C and C++ compilers, and have found hundreds of bugs.

