\section{Random Program Generation}\label{sec:ct-random}

\subsection{CSmith}\label{sec:ct-csmith}

\textsc{CSmith}~\cite{yang2011finding} is the most influential random program
generator for testing C compilers.  It generates random C programs that:
\begin{itemize}
\item Are syntactically valid (parse without errors).
\item Are free of undefined behavior (by construction).
\item Have well-defined, observable outputs (printed to stdout).
\item Exercise a wide range of language features.
\end{itemize}

CSmith uses a grammar-based approach, generating programs by randomly expanding
productions of a C-like grammar.  It employs several techniques to avoid
undefined behavior:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Volatile variables:} Used to prevent the compiler from optimizing
      away computations whose results are needed for checking.
\item \textbf{Safe arithmetic:} All arithmetic operations are wrapped to avoid
      overflow.
\item \textbf{Initialized variables:} All variables are initialized before use.
\item \textbf{Bounds checking:} All array accesses are within bounds.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{YarpGen}\label{sec:ct-yarpgen}

\textsc{YarpGen}~\cite{yarpgen2020} generates random C++ programs for testing
compilers.  It extends the CSmith approach to C++ features including:
\begin{itemize}
\item Classes and inheritance.
\item Templates.
\item Exception handling.
\item Operator overloading.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Other Generators}\label{sec:ct-other-generators}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textsc{Csmith-Redux}: A simplified version of CSmith for faster
      generation.
\item \textsc{Randstruct}: Generates programs with complex struct layouts.
\item \textsc{FuzzC}: A grammar-based fuzzer for C.
\item \textsc{Jigsaw}: A JavaScript fuzzer that generates programs exercising
      the full language specification.
\end{itemize}

