\section{Fuzzing Compilers}\label{sec:ct-fuzzing}

\subsection{Coverage-Guided Fuzzing}\label{sec:ct-coverage-fuzz}

Modern compiler fuzzing uses \emph{coverage-guided} techniques: the fuzzer
mutates programs to maximize the code coverage of the compiler itself (not the
generated code).  This approach, inspired by AFL~\cite{afl2020}, explores the
compiler's code space more efficiently than random generation.

\subsection{Compiler Fuzzing Pipeline}\label{sec:ct-fuzz-pipeline}

A typical compiler fuzzing pipeline consists of:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Generation:} Generate or mutate a test program.
\item \textbf{Compilation:} Compile the program with the target compiler.
\item \textbf{Oracle check:} Check for crashes (ICEs), assertion failures, or
      metamorphic relation violations.
\item \textbf{Feedback:} Use coverage information to guide future mutations.
\item \textbf{Reduction:} Reduce failing test cases to minimal reproducers.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{LangFuzz}\label{sec:ct-langfuzz}

\textsc{LangFuzz}~\cite{langfuzz2020} is a fuzzing framework for JavaScript
engines.  It uses a combination of random generation, mutation of existing test
cases, and coverage guidance to find bugs in V8, SpiderMonkey, and JavaScriptCore.

\subsection{GLeeFuzz}\label{sec:ct-gleefuzz}

\textsc{GLeeFuzz}~\cite{gleefuzz2020} fuzzes OpenGL drivers by generating
sequences of OpenGL API calls.  It uses a grammar-based approach to generate
valid API call sequences and checks for crashes and incorrect rendering.

