\section{Reducing Test Cases (C-Reduce)}\label{sec:ct-reduction}

\subsection{The Reduction Problem}\label{sec:ct-reduction-problem}

When a compiler bug is found, the test case that triggers it is typically large
(thousands of lines of generated code).  To make the bug report useful to
compiler developers, the test case must be \emph{reduced} to a minimal version
that still triggers the bug.

\begin{definition}[Test Case Reduction]\label{def:reduction}
Given a test case $T$ and a property $P$ (e.g., ``triggers a compiler crash''),
\emph{reduction} finds a test case $T'$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $T'$ satisfies $P$.
\item $|T'| \leq |T|$.
\item $T'$ is ``minimal'' in some sense (e.g., no further reduction is possible).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Delta Debugging}\label{sec:ct-delta-debug}

\emph{Delta debugging}~\cite{zeller2002simplifying} is the foundational
algorithm for test case reduction.  It works by iteratively removing chunks of
the test case and checking whether the property still holds.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Delta Debugging}\label{alg:delta-debug}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Test case $T$ (as a sequence of $n$ chunks), property $P$
\Ensure Reduced test case $T'$
\State $T' \gets T$
\State $g \gets n$ \Comment{Granularity}
\While{$g \geq 1$}
  \State Partition $T'$ into $g$ subsets $S_1, \ldots, S_g$
  \For{each $S_i$}
    \If{$P(T' \setminus S_i)$}
      \State $T' \gets T' \setminus S_i$
      \State $g \gets \max(g - 1, 2)$
      \State \textbf{continue} outer while loop
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \For{each pair $(S_i, S_j)$ with $i \neq j$}
    \If{$P(T' \setminus (S_i \cup S_j))$}
      \State $T' \gets T' \setminus (S_i \cup S_j)$
      \State $g \gets \max(g - 1, 2)$
      \State \textbf{continue} outer while loop
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State $g \gets g / 2$ \Comment{Reduce granularity}
\EndWhile
\State \Return $T'$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{C-Reduce}\label{sec:ct-creduce}

\textsc{C-Reduce}~\cite{regehr2012creduce} is the standard tool for reducing C
and C++ test cases.  It combines delta debugging with a collection of
\emph{reduction passes} that apply C-specific transformations:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Clang-based passes:} Use the Clang AST to perform semantic-aware
      reductions (e.g., removing unused functions, simplifying expressions).
\item \textbf{Text-based passes:} Apply text-level reductions (e.g., removing
      lines, replacing tokens with simpler alternatives).
\item \textbf{Custom passes:} Users can define custom reduction passes for
      domain-specific reductions.
\end{itemize}

C-Reduce can reduce a multi-thousand-line test case to a few dozen lines in
minutes, making the bug report tractable for compiler developers.

\begin{insight}
Test case reduction is an example of a more general principle in debugging:
\emph{minimize the context}.  A bug report with a 20-line test case is far more
useful than one with a 2000-line test case, even if both trigger the same bug.
Reduction tools like C-Reduce are essential for making automated testing
practical, as they bridge the gap between the large test cases generated by
fuzzers and the small, focused test cases needed for debugging.
\end{insight}

