\subsection{Impact}\label{sec:ct-impact}

Compiler testing tools have had a profound impact on compiler quality:
\begin{itemize}
\item CSmith has found over 400 bugs in GCC and Clang combined.
\item EMI testing has found hundreds of optimization bugs.
\item C-Reduce has become an essential tool in the compiler developer's toolkit.
\item Translation validation (see \cref{ch:translation-validation}) provides
      complementary guarantees.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Compiler testing has evolved through several phases.  Early compiler test suites
(1970s--1990s) consisted of hand-written test programs.  The introduction of
random program generation by CSmith~\cite{yang2011finding} in 2011 marked a
paradigm shift, enabling systematic exploration of the compiler's input space.
The development of metamorphic testing techniques~\cite{chen2018metamorphic}
and EMI testing further expanded the reach of automated compiler testing.
Today, compiler testing is an active area of research, with new tools and
techniques appearing regularly.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:ct-summary}

Compiler testing is a vital activity that ensures the correctness of the tools
on which all software development depends.  The key techniques---differential
testing, metamorphic testing, random program generation, and fuzzing---complement
each other and together provide broad coverage of the compiler's input space.

Active research areas include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Testing optimizing compilers:} Finding miscompilation bugs
      (harder than finding crash bugs).
\item \textbf{Testing language implementations:} Interpreters, type checkers,
      and other language tools.
\item \textbf{Automated test oracle generation:} Automatically determining the
      expected output of a program.
\item \textbf{Integration with formal methods:} Combining testing with
      translation validation and formal verification.
\end{itemize}
